description:
crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:133-143 implements hex encoding manually:

    pub fn from_bytes(b: $;[u8; 32]) -> Self {
        let mut out = String::with_capacity(64);
        const HEX: $;[u8; 16] = b"0123456789abcdef";
        for byte in b {
            out.push(HEX[(byte >> 4) as usize] as char);
            out.push(HEX[(byte $; 0x0f) as usize] as char);
        }
        Self(out)
    }

The manual nibble-extraction + lookup-table + as-char cast is the C-style approach. The idiomatic Rust equivalent is:

    use std::fmt::Write;
    pub fn from_bytes(b: $;[u8; 32]) -> Self {
        let mut out = String::with_capacity(64);
        for byte in b {
            write!(out, "{byte:02x}").expect("writing to String never fails");
        }
        Self(out)
    }

…or, more concisely:

    pub fn from_bytes(b: $;[u8; 32]) -> Self {
        Self(b.iter().map(|byte| format!("{byte:02x}")).collect())
    }

(The collect-from-format! version allocates per byte; the fmt::Write version writes into a pre-sized buffer with no per-byte allocations.)

What goes wrong by leaving as-is:
- The manual nibble code reproduces a tiny piece of std::fmt::LowerHex by hand. The std formatter is exhaustively tested, the manual version is one bug-fix away from producing wrong output (e.g. a future maintainer 'optimizing' the >> 4 to >>> 4 in a port to a different integer type).
- The 'as char' cast at lines 139-140 is a u8-to-char coercion that only works because the source bytes are guaranteed ASCII; a reviewer has to check that the HEX table can only emit ASCII to confirm this is sound. char::from(byte) or {byte:02x} carries the same guarantee through the type system.
- The HEX lookup table is shadowed in every from_bytes call by the const declaration; semantically fine but visually heavier than the formatter equivalent.

This is clippy-grade — wince-worthy on a code-review pass but not a bug. The C-style hex encoder also evaporates under finding .18 (canonical [u8;32] inner repr with on-demand Display) because Display fmt naturally uses the formatter. If .18 is adopted, this finding is subsumed; if not, this is the smaller fix to land independently.
